From: postmarketOS rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] net: ipa: tear the setup down when the modem crashes

When the modem crashes, ipa_modem_crashed() cleans up a great deal but never
undoes ipa_setup(). ipa->setup_complete stays true, the QMI handles stay
registered, and the command and exception endpoints and GSI stay set up, for a
modem that is gone.

It also masks the "setup ready" interrupt so the modem cannot call ipa_setup()
while that cleanup runs:

	/* Prevent the modem from triggering a call to ipa_setup() */
	ipa_smp2p_irq_disable_setup(ipa);

which sets smp2p->setup_disabled, and nothing ever clears it. Grep the tree:
three mentions of that field, the declaration, the kerneldoc line, and that one
assignment. ipa_smp2p_notify_reset(), which does run on
QCOM_SSR_BEFORE_POWERUP, only clears the two power notification bits.

So call ipa_teardown() at the end of ipa_modem_crashed(), and lift the mask on
QCOM_SSR_BEFORE_POWERUP, which arrives before the modem boots and so cannot
race the modem raising it. ipa_teardown() is no longer static for this.

Measured on a Motorola Moto G82 5G (SM6375), crashing the modem through
remoteproc's debugfs and watching whether the SoC survives the bring-up:

	before   4 resets in 5 bring-ups
	after    0 resets in 4 bring-ups

The resets left nothing behind: no kernel message in the four seconds between
"remote processor modem is now up" and the machine going down, an empty pstore,
and bootreason=watchdog. They did not happen at all with ipa.ko unloaded.

Two things this does not do, and one guess about why it works.

It does not bring the data path back. rmnet_ipa0 is unregistered when the modem
crashes and is still absent afterwards, because ipa_setup() never runs again:
there is no "IPA driver setup completed successfully" in the log and the
interrupt handler never fires. The modem does not re-raise setup ready after a
restart, so arming the interrupt is not sufficient, and quite possibly inert.
Restoring mobile data across a modem crash needs a different trigger and is
not attempted here.

Which means the teardown is almost certainly what stops the reset, since the
other half of this patch has no observable effect. That is an inference from
the interrupt handler never running, not something that has been tested by
applying the two halves separately.

Why an AP-side teardown should change what the modem does is not established.
The reset arrived with the AP silent and nothing written anywhere, which is
consistent with the modem escalating rather than the AP failing, but that is
not proven either.
--- a/drivers/net/ipa/ipa_smp2p.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ipa/ipa_smp2p.c
@@ -322,6 +322,28 @@
 	kfree(smp2p);
 }
 
+/* Re-arm the "setup ready" interrupt, so that a modem which has restarted can
+ * announce GSI ready and have it acted on. Without this the mask applied by
+ * ipa_smp2p_irq_disable_setup() on the modem's way down is never lifted, and
+ * IPA is finished until the next reboot.
+ */
+void ipa_smp2p_irq_enable_setup(struct ipa *ipa)
+{
+	struct ipa_smp2p *smp2p = ipa->smp2p;
+
+	if (!smp2p->setup_ready_irq)
+		return;
+
+	mutex_lock(&smp2p->mutex);
+
+	if (smp2p->setup_disabled) {
+		enable_irq(smp2p->setup_ready_irq);
+		smp2p->setup_disabled = false;
+	}
+
+	mutex_unlock(&smp2p->mutex);
+}
+
 void ipa_smp2p_irq_disable_setup(struct ipa *ipa)
 {
 	struct ipa_smp2p *smp2p = ipa->smp2p;
--- a/drivers/net/ipa/ipa_smp2p.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ipa/ipa_smp2p.h
@@ -38,6 +38,15 @@
 void ipa_smp2p_irq_disable_setup(struct ipa *ipa);
 
 /**
+ * ipa_smp2p_irq_enable_setup() - Re-enable the "setup ready" interrupt
+ * @ipa:	IPA pointer
+ *
+ * The inverse of ipa_smp2p_irq_disable_setup(), for the case where the modem
+ * has gone down and will come back and announce GSI ready again.
+ */
+void ipa_smp2p_irq_enable_setup(struct ipa *ipa);
+
+/**
  * ipa_smp2p_notify_reset() - Reset modem notification state
  * @ipa:	IPA pointer
  *
--- a/drivers/net/ipa/ipa_main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ipa/ipa_main.c
@@ -174,7 +174,7 @@
  * ipa_teardown() - Inverse of ipa_setup()
  * @ipa:	IPA pointer
  */
-static void ipa_teardown(struct ipa *ipa)
+void ipa_teardown(struct ipa *ipa)
 {
 	struct ipa_endpoint *exception_endpoint;
 	struct ipa_endpoint *command_endpoint;
--- a/drivers/net/ipa/ipa.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ipa/ipa.h
@@ -164,4 +164,10 @@
  */
 int ipa_setup(struct ipa *ipa);
 
+/**
+ * ipa_teardown() - Inverse of ipa_setup()
+ * @ipa:	IPA pointer
+ */
+void ipa_teardown(struct ipa *ipa);
+
 #endif /* _IPA_H_ */
--- a/drivers/net/ipa/ipa_modem.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ipa/ipa_modem.c
@@ -428,6 +428,15 @@
 	if (ret)
 		dev_err(dev, "error %d zeroing modem memory regions\n", ret);
 
+	/* Undo ipa_setup(), so that the modem's next announcement of GSI ready
+	 * is met with a clean setup rather than a second one layered on the
+	 * first. ipa_setup() is not idempotent: it would run gsi_setup() again,
+	 * enable endpoints that are already enabled and call ipa_qmi_setup() a
+	 * second time. This also clears ipa->setup_complete, which otherwise
+	 * stays true through a crash the driver has already cleaned up after.
+	 */
+	ipa_teardown(ipa);
+
 out_power_put:
 	(void)pm_runtime_put_autosuspend(dev);
 }
@@ -444,6 +453,11 @@
 		dev_info(dev, "received modem starting event\n");
 		ipa_uc_power(ipa);
 		ipa_smp2p_notify_reset(ipa);
+		/* The modem is about to boot and will announce GSI ready. That
+		 * interrupt was masked on the way down and nothing else lifts
+		 * it, so lift it here, before the modem can raise it.
+		 */
+		ipa_smp2p_irq_enable_setup(ipa);
 		break;
 
 	case QCOM_SSR_AFTER_POWERUP:
